# npm's only blocker.
#
# pnpm and yarn stop themselves — both read `packageManager` from package.json
# and refuse. npm honours that field only through Corepack, which is off by
# default, so without this flag npm is the one tool that would resolve, install
# and write a `package-lock.json` alongside `bun.lock`.
#
# `engine-strict` makes npm enforce the unsatisfiable `engines` ranges in
# package.json instead of warning about them. It is checked during RESOLUTION,
# before any lifecycle script, which is what makes it survive `--ignore-scripts`
# — and why those ranges read as an instruction rather than a version.
#
# Bun ignores both this file's `engine-strict` and the `engines` field, so the
# pair can only ever reject the tools this project does not use.
engine-strict=true
